<biblStruct xml:id="b0">
	<analytic>
		<title level="a" type="main">Comparison with State-of-Art. We compared our results with several recent state-of-the-art models applied to similar tweet classification tasks. Table 4 summarizes the performance metrics of these models</title>
	</analytic>
	<monogr>
		<title level="m">The MVEDL [16] and ETM-BiLSTM [2] approaches have reported accuracy of 91.7% and 90.50% respectively on the Covid-19 WNUT-20 dataset containing 10 K tweets. The Multi-Task model</title>
		<imprint/>
	</monogr>
</biblStruct>

<biblStruct xml:id="b1">
	<monogr>
		<title level="m" type="main">Our model achieves an accuracy of 88.40%, a precision of 87.47%, a recall of 88.64%, and an F1-score of 88.05% on a relatively small dataset of 6 K tweets only. In terms of F1-score, our model significantly outperforms TA-BERT [6], which reported an F1-score of 63.73% on 15 K Covid-19 tweets. This demonstrates the robustness and effectiveness of our</title>
		<imprint/>
	</monogr>
	<note>achieves an F1-score of 88.8% on a much larger disaster dataset of 166 K tweets It is to be noted that our model&apos;s F1-score of 87.61% is commendable given the significantly smaller dataset size. This highlights the capability of our model to extract meaningful information and accurately classify tweets even with limited data approach in classifying tweets as informative and non-informative, achieving high precision and recall. References</note>
</biblStruct>

